Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||07-22-99||03:45 PM||||I change my own oil. The reason: They won't fill the oil filter with oil before installing it. Have you ever started the car after you put a new filter on it and watched the oil pressure gauge? It doesn't move for quite a while if the filter is bone dry. That means no oil running through the motor. I fill the filter up and this doesn't happen. I t may be a little messy, but worth it(I think).
Z||000004||RICK||07-22-99||06:00 PM||||That car needs Nutri-System bad!!!
Z||000005||Patrick||07-22-99||11:16 PM||||<br>SCCA, I agree with you, and I've done the same procedure on all my other vehicles where the oil filter has mounted vertically (up and down). But have you ever tried to do that with an oil filter that mounts <b>horizontally</b> (sideways)? <p>Horizontal mount oil filters are such a nuisance when you can't help but slop filthy old oil all over the engine cradle during an oil change. My 2.5L ‘87 Fiero will attest to that!<p><br>
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||07-23-99||05:37 PM||||Patrick, my 94 S10 filter mounts sideways, I still fill it up. The filter holds 1 whole quart. That's alot of oil! I just screw it on as fast as I can and clean up the spill.<br>The filter on my S10 is behind a door on the skid plate. I wish my Fiero was that easy to get at. 
Z||000007||Patrick||07-23-99||10:25 PM||||<br>SCCA, you're crazy!<p>Either that or you're able to spin the filter on awfully quick. <IMG SRC="http://www.compucheap.com/ubb/smile.gif"> <p>
Z||000008||SCCA FIERO||07-24-99||01:05 AM||||Hmmmmm....I have to go with crazy.<p>Actually I let the oil soak in, so it doesn't come gushing out when it tips. It takes about 5 minutes to fill it, but it works.
